"evaporite",0.09],["loess",0.07]],"points":15,"scale":"medium","names":{"shale":"Shale and mudstone","limestone":"Limestone","sandstone":"Sandstone","evaporite":"Gypsum and salt","loess":"Loess"}},"large":false,"reading":{"climate":[["Grown here","Müller-Thurgau and Riesling sit in their sweet spot, so the wines can show the style they are known for."]]},"winkler":["Ia",""],"terms":[["Qualitätswein","German quality wine from one of the specified regions. It can be dry to sweet."],["Prädikatswein","Germany's top tier, graded by how ripe the grapes are at harvest. Unless the label says dry or off-dry, expect some sweetness."],["Kabinett","Light wine from fully ripe grapes of the main harvest, often semi-sweet with crisp acidity."],["Spätlese","Late harvest. Often sweeter and fruitier than Kabinett."],["Auslese","Select harvest from fully ripe or noble-rotted grapes."],["Beerenauslese (BA)","Overripe berries picked one by one, often with noble rot. Rich, sweet dessert wine."],["Trockenbeerenauslese (TBA)","Overripe berries shriveled on the vine, often with noble rot. Extremely rich and sweet. Trocken here means dried grapes, not a dry wine."],["Eiswein","Ice wine, from grapes frozen naturally on the vine. The must is at least as sweet as a Beerenauslese."],["Trocken, Halbtrocken","Dry and medium dry. Under EU rules a dry wine has no more than 4 g of sugar a liter, or a little more when its acidity is high. Medium dry has more sugar than that."],["VDP, Grosses Gewächs (GG)","The VDP is an association of about 200 estates with rules stricter than the law. GG is its top dry wine from its best sites."]],"style":{"notes":["Franken is known for powerful, dry Silvaner from chalky soils, slightly earthy and rustic.","Its best wines, mostly dry Spätlesen, are full bodied and can mature for six to ten years."]},"blurb":{"text":"A wine region in northern Bavaria. Silvaner and Müller-Thurgau lead the plantings, each with about a quarter of the vines, and Bacchus is third."},"study":true,"levels":[3],"summary":{"places":2,"kinds":[["PDO",2]],"grapes":[["Grüner Silvaner","white",2],["Weissburgunder","white",2],["Riesling","white",2]],"with_rules":2,"gst":[13.4,14.2],"best":[]},"sources":[{"name":"Wikidata sitelinks and Wikimedia page views","url":"https://www.wikidata.org"},{"name":"Candiago et al. 2022, A geospatial inventory of regulatory information for wine protected designations of origin in Europe, Scientific Data (product specifications as of 2021)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-022-01513-0"},{"name":"Fick and Hijmans 2017, WorldClim 2.1 monthly averages for 1970 to 2000, 2.5 minute grid (about 4.5 km)","url":"https://www.worldclim.org/data/worldclim21.html"},{"name":"Beck et al. 2023, High-resolution (1 km) Koppen-Geiger maps, Scientific Data (1991 to 2020)","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-023-02549-6"},{"name":"Winkler, Cook, Kliewer and Lider 1974, General Viticulture, University of California Press (heat summation regions)","url":null},{"name":"Jones 2010, Climate, grapes, and wine: structure and suitability in a variable and changing climate, VIII International Terroir Congress (which grapes suit which growing season temperatures)","url":"https://ives-openscience.eu/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/Climate_Grape_Wine_Jones.pdf"},{"name":"Anderson, Nelgen and Puga, Database of Regional, National and Global Winegrape Bearing Areas by Variety, 2000 to 2023, University of Adelaide (CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://doi.org/10.25909/32870405.v1"},{"name":"Wikipedia, German wine classification (what the label terms mean)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=German_wine_classification&oldid=1379409606"},{"name":"German Wine Act (Weingesetz), section 20 (what the label term means)","url":"https://www.gesetze-im-internet.de/weing_1994/__20.html"},{"name":"Wikipedia, Sweetness of wine (what the label terms mean)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Sweetness_of_wine&oldid=1377648282"},{"name":"Wikipedia, Verband 